OSX-Arch-Premise
This describes the first steps and the user experience in a counter-intuitive approach to what is considered a standard-operation or user-protocol.

To start off in a game we have an expectation of what is generally considered the rules of the game.
The game outlines or defines what is generally possible.
What makes the game as playable under a logical approach.
We understand that the interaction of a game is that each choice or command is therefore consequential or adequate in its presentations.
We are told for instance, that each action and response is black and white. Every question is given a direct answer and so on.
--
But at some point, does a game not provide the immediate expecation of its interaction. It does not simulate correctly.
There is a glitch or an unknown. An anamoly. A user error or lag-issue even.
That somewherein the logic/reason and chain/code, there was an unexpected result.
Maybe that the command or instruction actually did compute, but not in the way it was intended.
Maybe we wanted to login as a user, but inadvertedly, that deleted someone elses' player account. To make space. Or in a convuluted way, deleted a special item or zone or cause a player to experience lag themselves in a way that changed their experience.
This is a paradoxical affair.

--
This is a paradoxical nature of a gaming experience in the way that our intention or calculations are now in a quantum-possibility of different outcomes and results.
And what we expect out of the simple exchange taking place, is actually much more complicated in its logic/reasoning than initially assumed.
--That while the system may or may not be perfect, we are not fully in control either way.
--
At some point, the logic falls out, and we are left to the chaos, of what we break down as the most fundamental elements we put our trust in.
As a character might --fight a monster. The monster is supposed to give experience and loot. This helps the character level up and progress.
This may be an integral part of the storyline. It may be necessary for the character to play by the rules for this to happen.
But somewhere, all we have done is to expect a paradoxical-affair to play out, in the many ways that it could, and result in a distinction of itself.
--
And now, this we compare to all paradoxical affairs in such a scale:
That religions/cults and subjects like biology/science, simply are at the unnatural-whimsy of a quantum-paradox.
That we are playing a simulation designed to orchestrate to a perfect-machine or system, but it is still---as vulnerable as we are to it, the unknowns/anamolies of itself.
We can try to quantify and categorize as much as possible.
We can allow trade-offs and rationalizations.
We can say it is like carpentry or plumbing or electricians and technicians working out their problems in their own way.
We can attempt a vocational approach to help subsidize the workload and payoffs of these interactions.
We understand the events are meticulous and detailed, and we see overall how they attribute to the environment and player experience.
We see how our system may begin to appropriate itself, as its own intelligence or info-hazard that it may be.
We are personifying it in such a way we negotiate and interpret its own reality. (within a realm of accuracy that may or may not even come close)
---
For some, even that the game itself does not encompass their own aspirations, for instance one might decide to simply survey the stars, the skybox of the world, looking for artifacts or constellations. In some silly way, breaking the rules or intentions of the games or repurposing them for their own use.
